WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.800
Drain the thoracic duct of a rat for a few days, and the lymph runs short of

2
00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:11.800
lymphocytes. The usual view was that lymph nodes made those cells.

3
00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:16.280
If so, and they were released once, output should hold roughly steady.

4
00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:20.320
If the same cells go around a loop, removing them should empty it.

5
00:00:21.233 --> 00:00:26.333
Gowans left a cannula, a fine tube, in the thoracic duct of rats and collected lymph

6
00:00:26.333 --> 00:00:28.873
for days. Output fell progressively.

7
00:00:29.613 --> 00:00:33.993
When he returned the collected cells by vein, output recovered, and the returned

8
00:00:33.993 --> 00:00:35.833
cells turned up again in duct lymph.

9
00:00:36.573 --> 00:00:41.153
The cells were going around: from blood, through lymphoid tissue, into lymph, and

10
00:00:41.153 --> 00:00:42.153
back to blood.

11
00:00:44.667 --> 00:00:49.707
Labelled lymphocytes injected by vein into rats moved quickly into nodes, across the

12
00:00:49.707 --> 00:00:51.487
walls of high endothelial venules.

13
00:00:52.067 --> 00:00:56.287
A node has two entrances, afferent lymph and these venules, and one exit, the

14
00:00:56.287 --> 00:00:57.287
efferent lymphatic.

15
00:00:57.647 --> 00:01:01.567
In large animals at rest, more than 90 percent of lymphocytes in efferent lymph came

16
00:01:01.567 --> 00:01:02.567
in through the venules.

17
00:01:03.227 --> 00:01:05.267
L-selectin tethers the cell, and it rolls.

18
00:01:05.987 --> 00:01:11.407
A chemokine signal switches the integrin LFA-1, which grips ICAM-1 and ICAM-2.

19
00:01:11.407 --> 00:01:13.407
The cell stops, then crosses.

20
00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:16.170
Getting out needs a receptor.

21
00:01:16.810 --> 00:01:21.270
The lipid sphingosine 1-phosphate is high in blood and lymph, and low inside

22
00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:25.770
lymphoid tissue. Lymphocytes carry a receptor for it, S1P1.

23
00:01:26.290 --> 00:01:30.370
In mice whose blood-forming cells lacked it, mature T cells could not leave the

24
00:01:30.370 --> 00:01:35.150
thymus. Deficient T and B cells transferred into normal mice could not leave

25
00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:38.710
lymphoid organs. So the need lies in the lymphocyte itself.

26
00:01:39.783 --> 00:01:41.703
Exit sets how long a cell stays.

27
00:01:42.323 --> 00:01:45.443
The mean residence time is 1 divided by the exit rate constant.

28
00:01:46.063 --> 00:01:51.243
In a fitted rat model, the spleen's exit rate constant is 0.007 per minute.

29
00:01:51.803 --> 00:01:53.603
That gives about 2.4 hours.

30
00:01:54.303 --> 00:01:58.963
The subcutaneous nodes together hold a cell about 10 hours, and blood about 26

31
00:01:58.963 --> 00:02:04.483
seconds per pass. At steady state, relative to blood, the spleen holds 8 times as

32
00:02:04.483 --> 00:02:07.443
many cells, the subcutaneous nodes about 15.

33
00:02:08.383 --> 00:02:12.323
This keeps only those three; lung, liver and cell loss are left out.

34
00:02:13.267 --> 00:02:16.847
A node that drains a site of immune challenge changes its traffic.

35
00:02:17.487 --> 00:02:22.367
In mouse studies, its lymphocytes express CD69, which removes S1P1.

36
00:02:22.947 --> 00:02:25.507
Entry from blood rises, and for a time exit slows.

37
00:02:26.227 --> 00:02:30.767
With entry fixed and the exit rate constant halved, residence time doubles, and so

38
00:02:30.767 --> 00:02:31.767
does the number held.

39
00:02:32.207 --> 00:02:33.387
The shutdown is transient.

40
00:02:34.127 --> 00:02:38.027
Keep this: at steady state, cells held equal entry times residence.
